    "results_numbered": {
      "1": "Cartel deterrence: Fine and particularly Leniency deter cartel formation (comm. attempts 0.835 > 0.566 > 0.377; formation 0.716 > 0.315 > 0.178)",
      "2": "Cartel detection: Leniency substantially increases detection through self-reports (0.092 < 0.507; Reward 0.937)",
      "3": "Cartel recidivism: Leniency reduces re-formation after conviction (close to 40 percent immediate re-formation in Fine, much less in Leniency)",
      "4": "Average prices: Fine raises prices (5.349 > 4.917), Leniency leaves them almost unchanged (4.845)",
      "5": "Cartel prices: Fine (6.144) and Leniency (7.024) raise cartel prices relative to L-Faire (4.971)",
      "6": "Price deviations: Fine (0.424) and Leniency (0.373) reduce deviations relative to L-Faire (0.564)",
      "7": "Cartel prices and public reports: cartel price Fine 5.031 vs NoReport 3.553 (Rome)",
      "8": "Public reports as altruistic punishment: public reports given only the rival deviated higher in ReMatch (0.324) than Fine (0.197)",
      "reward": "Reward: formation 0.220 (above Leniency), reports 0.937, deviations 0.782 (above L-Faire), average price 3.973 and cartel price 5.339 below L-Faire; no pair took turns reporting (footnote 24)"
